<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krishna III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/939">939</a>–967</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> III was the last great emperor of the Rashtrakuta dynasty, ruling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> from 939 to 967. In 949 he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> army at Takkolam, where the Chola crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaditya">Rajaditya</a> was killed, and occupied the northern Chola lands for some years. He also campaigned in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and patronised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ponna">Ponna</a>. His death was followed by the rapid decline of the dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishna-iii.jpg" alt="Krishna III" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jugaari cross · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_dated_Shaka_868_(c.944-45_AD)_of_Rashtrakuta_empire_King_Krishna_III.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Krishna III held titles such as Akalavarsha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharajadhiraja">Maharajadhiraja</a>, Parameshvara and Shri Prithvivallabha. His queen was a Chedi princess, and his daughter Bijjabbe married a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga dynasty</a>. Early in his reign he killed the Western Ganga ruler Rachamalla II and made his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butuga-ii">Butuga II</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangavadi">Gangavadi</a>. He defeated his own relations, the Kalachuris of Tripuri, when they turned against him, and recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolar">Kolar</a> and Dharmapuri from the Banas and Vaidumbas, who had sheltered his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iv">Govinda IV</a>. Tondaimandalam was secured by 944.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butuga-ii">Butuga II</a> fought beside him at Takkolam, and the Atakur inscription records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> rewarded him with lands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a>. After the victory Krishna took tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> and the Chera ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, obtained the submission of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> and set up a pillar of victory at Ramesvaram, calling himself Conqueror of Kacci and Tanjai. Because his inscriptions are not found further south, historians argue that he fully controlled only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tondaimandalam">Tondaimandalam</a>. He also intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>, helping Badapa to the throne against Amma II.</p><p>In the north his Western Ganga vassal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marasimha-ii">Marasimha II</a> recovered Chitrakuta and Kalinjar from the Chandelas and defeated the Gurjara-Pratiharas; the Jura inscription near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabalpur">Jabalpur</a>, of about 964, is the northernmost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> record of the dynasty. Historians such as A. S. Altekar hold that Marasimha also fought the Paramara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyaka">Siyaka</a>, while K. N. Sethi finds no evidence of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Enmity with the Kalachuris of Tripuri weakened the empire towards the end of his rule, and he may have been reckless in granting large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> to his commanders. Some time before 965 he gave the province of Tardavadi, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Vijayapura</a> district, to his Chalukya feudatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii">Tailapa II</a>, a grant that later proved the undoing of the Rashtrakutas. Historian P. B. Desai describes him as Jain by faith. He kept up his family's patronage of Jain monks and temples, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ponna">Ponna</a> the title Ubhaya-Kavi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakravarti-sanskrit-term">Chakravarti</a>, and also supported the poet Gajankusha and the Apabhramsha poet Pushpadanta, author of the Mahapurana.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Krishna III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the following decades the Chandelas took the great fortress of Kalanjara, the Paramaras established themselves in Malwa, the Chaulukyas in Gujarat and the Kalachuris in central India, and further Rashtrakuta campaigns under <b>Krishna III</b> weakened the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…III marched north again and sacked Kannauj c. 916, humbling the Pratihara Mahipala I. The last great commander, <b>Krishna III</b>, allied with the Western Ganga Butuga II, defeated the Chola Empire at the Battle of Takkolam in 949, where the Chola prince Rajaditya was killed on his elephant,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
